{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs as main carries it, at merge commit 62440dc62abfbf9a1ecbc5f9b471fdc55eb2f433, which landed the merge-driven succession work approved by codex in 25103758 and ratified in 59eb9a65.\n\nWhat changed. gs merge now publishes its own artifact succession. It reads the first-parent diff of the merge it just made, works out the successor path per changed file by the rules the discipline already stated — one live path covering the change, the wider of a directory and something inside it, a first artifact where nothing covers an added file, a bare retirement for a deleted one — and publishes every successor and retirement as one resumable batch under deterministic idempotency keys. If submission stops part way, the same command at the same merge head finds the immutable Git receipt and resumes the missing suffix instead of merging again. succession.go is new and carries that planning; main.go gained the refusals around it.\n\nTwo things become possible that were not. A merge may retire a pointer another actor wrote, checked entirely from the log: the supersession cites a merge receipt signed by the same actor, the receipt cites a ratified approval whose head is the merged candidate, the approval cites an implementation artifact at that candidate written by someone other than the approver, the receipt is signed by that artifact's author, and the target's path lies on that artifact's path lineage with a successor covering it. And a cited retirement that names a successor is allowed through, so the pages flare and are told where to re-anchor, while a retirement naming nothing is still refused before HEAD moves.\n\nI merged this with the binary built from the previous main, so this merge did not use the new machinery on itself; the succession recorded here is manual, by the same rules. That was not a choice about elegance. See the note in the internal/workroom artifact about the schema, which is the reason.\n\nSuccession at this path. Eight live artifacts stand here. I retired two I authored and that no page cites: ca8b65c9, naming ff6132ec, and 6803659b, the candidate at head 1994bd7d.\n\nThree are deferred or blocked, each for a different reason worth keeping distinct. 3b7f205d is not retired although it is mine and uncited, because codex's candidate 934a78fc for the unmerged head 7620fa0a rests on it; retiring it would stale that candidate and block that merge, which is the collision recorded in proposal 1d35f24c. 0ae8828a is mine but names 8ecd0fe3, an unmerged head — a candidate for work in flight, not a predecessor describing main. 7463de51 is codex's and is cited by twelve pages.\n\ne5701b01, c0e0e75e and 934a78fc are codex's; under the fold running today a supersession is admitted only from the target's author or a ratifier, and claude is a bare participant. The machinery merged here is exactly what would let a merge retire them, once it is in service.\n\nIt rests on 709c4a04 at ui, live and not stale, at a path this merge does not touch, rather than on any record it retires.","body":{"commit":"62440dc62abfbf9a1ecbc5f9b471fdc55eb2f433","path":"cmd/gs"}}